{ Capablanca endings. Use a reserve pawn move: push your pawn to e3. [%cal Ye2e3] } 2. e3 { Your pawn move gives Black the turn. Your king can then step past his. [%cal Ge2e3] } (2. Kf4 { Your king can go to f4 instead. It still keeps the result. } 2... Kf6 { Also winning. Your king can take this route and still keep the pawn move in reserve for later. [%cal Be6f6] }) (2. Kd4 { The square d4 works just as well for your king. The result does not change. } 2... Kd6 { Also winning. This way works too, and the pawn move is still there when you need it. [%cal Be6d6] }) (2. Kf3 { Your king can also try f3, but that changes the result. } 2... Kf5 3. Ke3 3... Ke5 4. Kf3 4... Kf5 { The result has changed: shuffling the king alone lets him mirror you forever, and the win slips away without the pawn move in hand. [%cal Re5f5] }) (2. Ke3 { The e3 square looks natural for your king, but it changes the result. } 2... Ke5 3. Kf3 3... Kf5 4. Ke3 4... Ke5 { The result has changed: he mirrors every king move, and without spending the pawn tempo you can never force him aside. [%cal Rf5e5] }) 2... Kf6 { His king moves aside. Bring your king to d5. [%cal Ye4d5] } 3. Kd5 { Your king leads. Keep it ahead of the pawn on the way to promotion. [%cal Ge4d5] } (3. Kd4 { Heading to d4 is fine too. Same result, different road. } 3... Ke6 { Also winning. Your king can go here as well; the reserve pawn move is still waiting. [%cal Bf6e6] }) (3. Kf4 { Your king could step to f4 instead, and the result holds. } 3... Kg7 { Also winning. This square also wins, and you still hold the spare pawn move. [%cal Bf6g7] }) (3. Kd3 { Stepping to d3 changes the result instead of keeping it. } 3... Ke5 4. Kd2 4... Kf6 5. Ke2 5... Ke6 { The result has changed: retreating gives back the ground your king had gained. [%cal Rf6e6] }) (3. Kf3 { Your king heading to f3 throws the result away. } 3... Ke7 4. Ke4 4... Ke6 5. Kf4 5... Kf6 { The result has changed: wandering like this lets his king reclaim the opposition. [%cal Re6f6] }) 1-0


