{ The pawn is close to promotion. Which must lead now: your king or your pawn? } 8. Ke6 { Your king takes the key square. Pushing the pawn first would give away the win. [%cal Gf6e6] } (8. e6 { The pawn gets ahead of your king. The opposing king can stay in its way. } 8... Kf8 9. Kg6 9... Ke8 10. Kf5 10... Ke7 { The king blocks the pawn. You needed to lead with your king. [%cal Re8e7] }) (8. Kf5 { The square f5 seems fine, but the result changes here. } 8... Kf7 9. Kg4 9... Kg6 10. Kf4 10... Kf7 { The result has changed: his king reaches the key square first this way. [%cal Rg6f7] }) (8. Kg6 { This king move to g6 is a step too far. The result changes. } 8... Ke7 9. Kg5 9... Ke6 10. Kf4 10... Kd7 { This move hands him the promotion route, and the result has changed. [%cal Re6d7] }) (8. Kg5 { Try g5, and the result flips against you. } 8... Kf7 9. Kf4 9... Kg6 10. Kg4 10... Kg7 { The result changes here, because your king arrives one move too late. [%cal Rg6g7] }) 8... Kf8 { His king yields a square. Keep the promotion route under your control. } 9. Kd7 { Your king controls the way forward. Bring the pawn up behind it. [%cal Ge6d7] } (9. Kf6 { This route through f6 keeps the result exactly the same. } 9... Ke8 { Also winning. Your king still controls the promotion route from here. [%cal Bf8e8] }) (9. Kd6 { Another good square, d6. The result stays with you. } 9... Kf7 { This wins too. Your king still reaches the key square first. [%cal Bf8f7] }) (9. Kd5 { Your king to d5 looks reasonable, but it changes the result. } 9... Ke7 10. Ke4 10... Kf7 11. Ke3 11... Ke6 { You lose control of the route this way, and the result has changed. [%cal Rf7e6] }) (9. Kf5 { Going to f5 instead costs you the result. } 9... Kf7 10. Kg4 10... Kg6 11. Kf4 11... Kf7 { The result has changed: the promotion square needed your king there first. [%cal Rg6f7] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Time the two-square pawn advance"]
