{ Your f pawn has advanced. Bring your king close enough to help the majority. } 2. Ke5 { Your king supports the advance. The g and h pawns can join in. [%tail] [%cal Gd4e5] } (2. g5 { Your pawn can advance to g5 instead. It still keeps the result. } 2... Kd6 { Also winning. Your king still comes forward to support the majority. [%cal Be7d6] }) (2. Kd3 { Your king can take the d3 path instead. Nothing changes. } 2... Kf6 { This wins too. Your king still reaches the pawns in time to help. [%cal Be7f6] }) (2. Kd5 { The d5 square is equally good for your king. The result stands. } 2... Kf6 { Still winning: your king supports the majority just as well from here. [%cal Be7f6] }) (2. Ke4 { Going to e4 costs you nothing. The result is unchanged. } 2... Kd6 { Also winning. Your king still gets there in time to help the pawns through. [%cal Be7d6] }) (2. f6+ { Your pawn can also push to f6, but that changes the result. } 2... Kxf6 { His king takes, keeping the pawns connected. Your extra pawn is gone. The ending draws. [%cal Re7f6] }) (2. h5 { The h5 square looks tempting for the pawn, but it changes the result. } 2... Kf6 3. Ke4 3... h6 4. Kf3 4... Kf7 { The result has changed: the h pawn ran ahead of your king, and by the time it arrives Black has organized a defense. [%cal Rf6f7] }) 2... Kd7 { He abandons the king side; three pawns cannot be stopped by a king alone. [%cal Ge7d7] } 3. g5 { A second pawn joins the advance. [%cal Gg4g5] } 3... Kc6 { His king keeps marching toward nothing in particular. [%cal Gd7c6] } 4. h5 { All three pawns are rolling forward together. [%cal Gh4h5] } 4... Kb5 { He is three ranks away and cannot get back in time. [%cal Gc6b5] } 5. f6 { Your pawn cannot be caught now. [%cal Gf5f6] } 5... Ka4 { His king gives up the chase for good. [%cal Gb5a4] } 6. fxg7 { The pawn reaches the seventh row. [%cal Gf6g7] } 6... Ka3 { He can only watch it queen. [%cal Ga4a3] } 7. g8=Q { Queen! Your three pawns turned into a new queen while his king was still crossing the board. [%csl Gg8] } 1-0


