{ Both sides want a queen. Choose the file that will control his promotion square. } 4. a6 { You ignore the pawn and stay on the a column. A queen on a8 will guard h1. [%cal Ga5a6] } (4. axb6 { You take the pawn, but change the square where your new queen will appear. } 4... h3 5. b7 5... h2 6. b8=Q 6... h1=Q { Both pawns promote. Your queen no longer controls his promotion square. [%cal Rh2h1] }) (4. Ka4 { Try a4, and the result flips against you. } 4... bxa5 5. Kxa5 5... Kg6 6. Kb4 6... h3 { The result has changed: promoting elsewhere, your new queen no longer guards h1. [%cal Rh4h3] }) (4. Kb4 { Your king to b4 looks reasonable, but it changes the result. } 4... bxa5+ 5. Kxa5 5... Kg6 6. Kb4 6... h3 { This loses the point. A queen born anywhere but a8 cannot watch h1 in time. [%cal Rh4h3] }) (4. Kc4 { Going to c4 instead costs you the result. } 4... bxa5 5. Kb5 5... Kf6 6. Kxa5 6... Ke5 { The result changes here, since your queen never gets to guard h1 from a8. [%cal Rf6e5] }) 4... h3 { He races his pawn. Moving his king would only delay your win. } 5. a7 { Your pawn is one step away. Keep the promotion diagonal in mind. [%cal Ga6a7] } (5. Kc4 { The c4 square is tempting, but the result changes there. } 5... h2 6. a7 6... h1=Q 7. Kb5 7... Qa8 { Without a8, your new queen cannot stop his pawn queening on h1, and the result changes. [%cal Rh1a8] }) (5. Ka3 { Your king's route through a3 changes the result. } 5... h2 6. Ka4 6... Kf7 7. Kb5 7... h1=Q { The result has changed: h1 is left unguarded, and his pawn queens safely. [%cal Rh2h1] }) (5. Kb4 { This time b4 is the wrong square. The result changes. } 5... h2 6. Kb5 6... Kf6 7. Kxb6 7... h1=Q { This gives up the a8 diagonal, and the result has changed. [%cal Rh2h1] }) (5. Ka4 { Your king can also try a4, but that changes the result. } 5... h2 6. Kb5 6... Kf6 7. Kxb6 7... h1=Q { The result changes: your queen needed to be born on a8 to control h1. [%cal Rh2h1] }) 5... h2 { He keeps racing. Which promotion controls his queening square? } 6. a8=Q { Queen! From a8 she guards h1, so his pawn cannot promote safely. [%cal Ga7a8] } (6. Kc4 { The c4 square looks natural for your king, but it changes the result. } 6... h1=Q 7. Kb5 7... Qa8 8. Kxb6 8... Kh6 { This way your queen misses h1 entirely, and the result has changed. [%cal Rg7h6] }) 1-0


[Event "Capablanca endings: Use the squares the king abandons"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/5k2/5P2/6p1/4K1P1/8/8/8/8_b_-_-_0_8"]
