{ Your f pawn has offered itself. Find a route for your king after the capture. } 8... Kxf7 { He takes the pawn. Can your king use the squares he left behind? } 9. Kd6 { Your king can circle toward g6. The pawn sacrifice bought that route. [%tail] [%cal Ge5d6] } (9. Kd5 { Your king can go to d5 instead. It still keeps the result. } 9... Ke7 { Also winning. The f pawn has already pulled his king away from g6, and that gain still stands. [%cal Bf7e7] }) (9. Kf4 { Stepping to f4 changes the result instead of keeping it. } 9... Kf8 10. Ke5 10... Ke7 11. Kd5 11... Kd7 { The result has changed: this route lets his king back to defend g6 in time. [%cal Re7d7] }) (9. Kd4 { Your king heading to d4 throws the result away. } 9... Ke6 10. Ke4 10... Kd6 11. Kd4 11... Ke6 { This move gives back the ground the f pawn sacrifice had won, and the result has changed. [%cal Rd6e6] }) (9. Ke4 { The square e4 seems fine, but the result changes here. } 9... Ke6 10. Kf4 10... Kd5 11. Kg4 11... Ke5 { The result changes here, because his king can now cover g6 after all. [%cal Rd5e5] }) 9... Kf8 { He hurries back to shield the king side. [%cal Gf7f8] } 10. Ke6 { Your king slips past him on the other side. [%cal Gd6e6] } 10... Kg7 { He tries to get back in front of it. [%cal Gf8g7] } 11. Ke7 { Another step through the gap the sacrifice opened. [%cal Ge6e7] } 11... Kg8 { He shuffles back again, still a move too slow. [%cal Gg7g8] } 12. Kf6 { Your king reaches the square his own pawn once stood on. [%cal Ge7f6] } 12... Kh7 { He guards the g pawn as best he can. [%cal Gg8h7] } 13. Kf7 { Your king takes the square right beside the pawn. [%cal Gf6f7] } 13... Kh8 { He cannot hold both the pawn and the corner. [%cal Gh7h8] } 14. Kxg6 { Your king captures the last pawn. Only your g pawn is left on the board, and his lone king cannot stop it queening. [%cal Gf7g6] } 1-0


